{"id":2992,"date":"2021-06-16T08:44:13","date_gmt":"2021-06-16T15:44:13","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=2992"},"modified":"2024-09-01T13:43:58","modified_gmt":"2024-09-01T20:43:58","slug":"camera","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/camera\/","title":{"rendered":"1.9: Camera"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214ec6f90b\" name=\"6a2214ec6f90b\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>How does Zumi see?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Self-driving cars need a lot more than just obstacle detection sensors. Human drivers have eyes and ears that help us see potential dangers up ahead that a proximity detector might not be able to detect. We can also tell the different between pedestrians, cyclists, and other cars. What else do self-driving cars need to navigate our world?[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214ec6fa23\" name=\"6a2214ec6fa23\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Camera blocks<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Open up the red &#8220;Camera&#8221; menu to see all of the functions you can use. The most important camera block is the &#8220;import camera&#8221; block. It <strong>always<\/strong> has to be in all of your programs if you want to use the camera. This code will set up all of the background code for using the camera. Add that block to your workspace[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12292&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12293&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214ec6fa75\" name=\"6a2214ec6fa75\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Take a selfie!<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]You are going to write a program to take a selfie using Zumi&#8217;s camera. Before writing the code, remember where the camera is so that you can line up your face![\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2014&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214ec6fadf\" name=\"6a2214ec6fadf\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Selfie code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]There are three functions that you need to know for taking pictures with Zumi.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2017&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Before taking a picture, you will need to turn on the camera with the start camera block. You cannot take an image without the camera stream! The red light will indicate the camera is on.<\/li>\n<li>Second, create a variable called &#8220;image&#8221; and attach it to the take picture block. This will save the image data to that variable.<\/li>\n<li>Finally, don&#8217;t forget to turn off the camera! Use the close camera block when you are done using the camera. If you forget to turn off the camera and you leave Blockly, it can cause errors in other projects and drain your battery.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Here is what the final code looks like. <span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">This will take a <\/span><b>while<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> to run because it takes Zumi a few moments to import and start the camera. Be patient and say cheese!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12294&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214ec6fb27\" name=\"6a2214ec6fb27\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Show image<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]When you ran the code, you probably noticed the camera LED turn on and off but you didn&#8217;t see the picture. In order to see the picture, we need to add one more line of code. Add the show image block and pass in the image you saved earlier in the variable.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12295&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214ec6fb70\" name=\"6a2214ec6fb70\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What do images look like to a computer?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Zumi doesn&#8217;t need to &#8220;see&#8221; the image like we do to process it because all she sees is numbers! To show what we mean, print the variable using the print block instead of showing the image.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12296&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]You probably saw lots of numbers! You will learn more about\u00a0<strong>pixels\u00a0<\/strong>later, but each set of three numbers represents one pixel. For humans, reading pictures like this is hard, but for Zumi it&#8217;s how she can process and identify images![\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214ec6fbb6\" name=\"6a2214ec6fbb6\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Video<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Although a video looks seamless, a video is actually a series of pictures one after the other. The images are shown so fast that you normally do not notice a difference. However, you will notice the difference here because it takes time to display each picture. In order to display a &#8220;video&#8221;, take and display pictures inside of a loop. Try out the code below.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12297&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Notice that the import, start, and close camera blocks are outside of the loop. You only need to import and start the camera once! If you place these blocks inside the loop, your program would have to turn on and off the camera each time it goes through the loop.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214ec6fbfb\" name=\"6a2214ec6fbfb\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Review<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In this lesson, you learned how to use the camera blocks to take single pictures and multiple pictures in a loop. Remember to save your images in variables so you can show the images later. In the next lessons, you will learn how to use the camera blocks to detect signs, faces, and QR codes to build even more complex programs.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2938&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 9 How does Zumi see? [vc_column_text]Self-driving cars need a lot more than just obstacle detection sensors. Human drivers have eyes and ears that help us see potential dangers&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/camera\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":2898,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":2878,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1789,1503,1482,514,853,996,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2108,2236,2238,2237,2156,2122,2239,2155,2230],"class_list":["post-2992","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-blockly","lesson-tag-camera","lesson-tag-does","lesson-tag-how","lesson-tag-intermediate","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-see","lesson-tag-sensor","lesson-tag-zumi","module-junior","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/2992","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":11,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/2992\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":12298,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/2992\/revisions\/12298"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/2898"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=2992"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=2992"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=2992"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}